DATA.lessons.push({id:"lesson-3",num:3,title:"Layout: Row, Column & Container",description:"Arrange UI with Row, Column and Container. Alignment, padding, and the classic overflow bug.",markdown:"# Lesson 3: Layout — Row, Column & Container\n\n## How Flutter lays things out\n\nFlutter layout is beautifully simple: widgets are arranged by **parent widgets whose only job is positioning**. Want things side by side? Put them in a `Row`. Stacked vertically? A `Column`. Need padding or a background color? Wrap it in a `Container`.\n\nYou rarely position things with x/y coordinates. You **compose layout widgets**, and Flutter measures everything for you.\n\n## Row and Column\n\n`Row` arranges its children **horizontally**. `Column` arranges them **vertically**. Both take a `children` list:\n\n```dart\nColumn(\n  children: const [\n    Text('First'),\n    Text('Second'),\n    Text('Third'),\n  ],\n)\n```\n\nTwo properties control alignment, and they're worth memorizing:\n\n- **`mainAxisAlignment`** — alignment along the main direction (horizontal for `Row`, vertical for `Column`).\n- **`crossAxisAlignment`** — alignment across it.\n\nSo in a `Column`, `mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center` centers children **vertically**, while `crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.center` centers them **horizontally**. Common values: `start`, `center`, `end`, `spaceBetween`, `spaceEvenly`.\n\n```dart\nRow(\n  mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.spaceEvenly,\n  children: const [\n    Icon(Icons.star),\n    Icon(Icons.favorite),\n    Icon(Icons.thumb_up),\n  ],\n)\n```\n\n## Container: the styling workhorse\n\n`Container` is the widget you reach for when you need **padding, margins, background color, borders, or a fixed size**:\n\n```dart\nContainer(\n  padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),\n  margin: const EdgeInsets.symmetric(vertical: 8),\n  color: Colors.blue,\n  child: const Text(\n    'Inside a container',\n    style: TextStyle(color: Colors.white),\n  ),\n)\n```\n\n`EdgeInsets.all(16)` means 16 pixels on every side. `EdgeInsets.symmetric` lets you set vertical and horizontal separately. You'll type `EdgeInsets` a hundred times — it becomes muscle memory fast.\n\n## Putting it together: a simple card\n\n```dart\nContainer(\n  padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),\n  margin: const EdgeInsets.all(16),\n  color: Colors.white,\n  child: Row(\n    children: const [\n      Icon(Icons.person, size: 48),\n      SizedBox(width: 16),\n      Column(\n        crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,\n        children: [\n          Text('Aarav Sharma', style: TextStyle(fontSize: 20)),\n          Text('Flutter Learner'),\n        ],\n      ),\n    ],\n  ),\n)\n```\n\nA `Container` holds a `Row`; the `Row` holds an icon, a `SizedBox` spacer, and a `Column` of two texts. Layout widgets nesting inside layout widgets — that's 90% of real Flutter UI work.\n\n## The classic beginner error: overflow\n\nIf a `Row`'s children are wider than the screen, Flutter shows the infamous **yellow-and-black striped overflow warning**. The fix is usually wrapping children in `Expanded`, which tells a child to take up the remaining space:\n\n```dart\nRow(\n  children: const [\n    Expanded(child: Text('This text takes all remaining space')),\n    Icon(Icons.arrow_forward),\n  ],\n)\n```\n\nThe lesson quiz checks that you know how to prevent this bug.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Main axis** — the direction a Row/Column lays children out in.\n- **Cross axis** — the direction perpendicular to the main axis.\n- **Expanded** — forces a child of a Row/Column to fill remaining space.\n- **EdgeInsets** — describes padding and margins.\n",pdf:"data:application/octet-stream;base64,",quizId:"quiz-3"});